        "bullets": [
            "Trump is demanding compensation from Iran as U.S.-Iran talks on reopening the Strait of Hormuz continue, per Al Jazeera.",
            "Iran says the strait will remain closed until the U.S. meets its demands; the U.S. asserts ships are still moving, leaving the transit picture contested.",
            "Brent stood at $87.77 (+3.98% over 24h) as of 02:53 UTC; 298 vessels were holding position away from berth at that time."
        ],
        "date": "2026-08-11",
        "generatedAt": "2026-08-11T02:53:22.462Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains in a contested closure posture, with the U.S. and Iran trading escalating demands rather than converging on a reopening agreement. Al Jazeera reports that Trump is now demanding compensation from Iran as talks between the two sides continue, a position corroborated by multiple outlets. Iran has countered by insisting the strait will not be safe until the U.S. ends what Tehran describes as a blockade of Iranian ports, a claim carried by Shoreline Media Group relaying an Iranian statement, not independently confirmed. The U.S. asserts ships are still moving, leaving the physical transit picture genuinely disputed; this site's carrier posture verdict is a cautious closed. The most recent PortWatch published day, 2026-08-02, recorded 2 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day. Separately, port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides totalled 418 vessels in the most recent 24 hours, a distinct methodology that cannot be directly compared to the PortWatch transit figure. As of 02:53 UTC, Brent stood at $87.77, up 3.98% over 24 hours. The site's Crisis Pressure index remains in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor; the Escalation Forecast sits in the elevated band and is rising, with Polymarket escalation contracts the leading driver. The two indices diverge in direction, a split worth watching.",
